          <li><strong>The Financial Penalty:</strong> In states where credit scoring is legal, drivers with "Poor" credit pay an average of <strong>$1,540 more per year (76% higher)</strong> for car insurance than drivers with "Excellent" credit—even with identical spotless driving records.</li>
          <li><strong>The 4 Banned States:</strong> <strong>California, Massachusetts, and Hawaii</strong> completely prohibit insurers from using credit scores to set auto rates. <strong>Michigan</strong> restricts the use of credit information under its 2020 auto insurance reform laws.</li>
