      <h2>Why Do Insurance Companies Look at Credit Scores?</h2>
      <p>
        Insurers do not pull your credit report to determine whether you can pay your monthly premium bill. Rather, decades of actuarial studies—including landmark reports by the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the University of Texas—demonstrated an overwhelming statistical correlation between credit file stability and loss ratios.
      </p>
      <p>
        Actuaries discovered that consumers who manage their credit lines with high discipline statistically file fewer insurance claims, file claims of smaller dollar amounts, and are significantly less likely to inflate or fabricate claim damages.
      </p>

      <h2>Credit-Based Insurance Score (CBIS) vs. Traditional FICO Score</h2>
      <p>
        Your insurance score is not the credit score a mortgage officer or auto lender pulls. While both rely on data from Equifax, Experian, or TransUnion, their weighting algorithms differ substantially:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">What Goes into a Credit-Based Insurance Score:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Payment History (~40%):</strong> Consistent, on-time payments across all trade lines over multi-year periods.</li>
          <li><strong>Outstanding Debt & Credit Utilization (~30%):</strong> The percentage of revolving credit limits currently in use.</li>
          <li><strong>Credit History Length (~15%):</strong> The age of your oldest trade line and average account maturity.</li>
          <li><strong>Pursuit of New Credit (~10%):</strong> Recent hard inquiries for personal credit cards and consumer loans.</li>
          <li><strong>Credit Mix (~5%):</strong> Balanced combinations of revolving credit cards and installment loans.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--color-text-muted);">
          <strong>What Is Strictly Excluded by Federal Law:</strong> Race, religion, nationality, marital status, gender, ZIP code, personal salary, bank account balances, and investment assets.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>The 4 States That Ban or Restrict Credit Scoring</h2>
      <p>
        State legislators and consumer protection groups in several jurisdictions argued that credit scoring unfairly penalizes low-income communities. As a result, four states prohibit or tightly constrain the practice:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>California:</strong> Proposition 103 mandates that auto rates be determined primarily by driving safety record, annual mileage, and years of driving experience. Credit scoring is 100% illegal.</li>
        <li><strong>Massachusetts:</strong> State regulations forbid insurance carriers from accessing credit information when rating personal auto policies.</li>
        <li><strong>Hawaii:</strong> State insurance statutes prohibit credit scoring in auto underwriting.</li>
        <li><strong>Michigan:</strong> The 2020 auto insurance reform package strictly barred carriers from using credit scores, education levels, or zip codes as direct rating factors.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>4 Steps to Lower Your Insurance Costs If You Have Poor Credit</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Shop Carriers with Mild Credit Penalties:</strong> Insurers weight credit differently. GEICO and Nationwide consistently exhibit lower percentage surcharges for credit-challenged drivers compared to State Farm or Allstate.</li>
        <li><strong>Lower Credit Utilization Below 30%:</strong> Paying down revolving credit balances improves your insurance score faster than almost any other metric.</li>
        <li><strong>Request an Insurance Re-Score at Renewal:</strong> If your credit score improved over the past six months, proactively contact your insurance agent to request a "policy re-score." Insurers do not automatically lower your rate mid-term without a consumer re-score request.</li>
        <li><strong>Adopt Telematics Driving Monitoring:</strong> If your credit is poor but your driving habits are exemplary, enrolling in a usage-based telematics program (DriveSafe&Save, Snapshot, Drivewise) allows you to prove your low risk through real-time driving sensors.</li>
      </ol>

